WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.127
Foundation models have transformed language and
vision, yet tabular data,

00:00:05.207 --> 00:00:08.158
the workhorse of data science, was left behind.

00:00:08.238 --> 00:00:10.415
Every table has a different schema,

00:00:10.495 --> 00:00:13.897
so a model trained on one rarely transfers to another.

00:00:13.977 --> 00:00:15.832
This paper introduces UniTabE,

00:00:15.912 --> 00:00:20.088
a universal pretraining protocol that handles any
table uniformly.

00:00:20.168 --> 00:00:23.699
It encodes each cell with a small module called TabUnit,

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:28.213
refines the table with a Transformer, and adapts
through text prompts.

00:00:28.293 --> 00:00:32.340
Pretrained on thirteen billion Kaggle samples, it
beats XGBoost.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.994
Tabular data underpins applications like stock
prediction, real-estate forecasting,

00:00:38.074 --> 00:00:39.219
and credit scoring.

00:00:39.299 --> 00:00:43.735
Yet unlike text and images, it has no widely
adopted foundation model.

00:00:43.815 --> 00:00:48.831
The reason: tables come in endless schemas,
different column names, data types,

00:00:48.911 --> 00:00:53.088
and counts, so a model trained on one cannot be
reused on another.

00:00:53.168 --> 00:00:57.926
Existing methods either flatten tables into text,
losing numerical meaning,

00:00:58.006 --> 00:01:01.086
or assume train and test share a fixed structure.

00:01:01.166 --> 00:01:03.860
Both block knowledge transfer across tasks.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.999
The promise of pretraining is that a model learns
general knowledge once from huge unlabeled data,

00:01:10.079 --> 00:01:11.906
then transfers it to many tasks.

00:01:11.986 --> 00:01:15.243
The authors ask whether this recipe can work for tables.

00:01:15.323 --> 00:01:20.009
Three ingredients are needed: a way to represent
any table regardless of schema,

00:01:20.089 --> 00:01:23.406
a training framework flexible enough for many objectives,

00:01:23.486 --> 00:01:26.504
and a data source large enough to pretrain at scale.

00:01:26.584 --> 00:01:30.556
Together, one tabular model could handle
classification, regression,

00:01:30.636 --> 00:01:35.382
missing-value imputation, zero-shot prediction, and
tables that grow new columns,

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:37.110
without redesigning per task.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:42.300
At UniTabE's heart is a module called TabUnit that
handles one cell at a time.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.563
It treats each cell as a key-value pair: the column
name is the key,

00:01:46.643 --> 00:01:47.943
the content the value.

00:01:48.023 --> 00:01:53.146
The name is embedded and mean-pooled, and a
data-type embedding, marking numerical,

00:01:53.226 --> 00:01:56.407
categorical, or textual, is fused in through a gate,

00:01:56.487 --> 00:02:00.795
so a salary column is handled whether its values
are numbers or words.

00:02:00.875 --> 00:02:04.808
A linking layer injects the column vector into each
value token,

00:02:04.888 --> 00:02:07.441
so attention links values to their column.

00:02:07.521 --> 00:02:11.892
All cell vectors, plus a CLS token, pass through a
Transformer encoder.

00:02:11.972 --> 00:02:14.149
A deliberately shallow LSTM decoder,

00:02:14.229 --> 00:02:18.099
guided by a free-form prompt like fill in missing
value salary,

00:02:18.179 --> 00:02:20.356
generates the answer token by token.

00:02:20.436 --> 00:02:24.933
Keeping the decoder weak forces most knowledge into
the reusable encoder.

00:02:25.013 --> 00:02:28.820
Pretraining mixes multi-cell masking and
contrastive learning.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:30.390
To pretrain at scale,

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:35.710
the team assembled a massive tabular dataset from
Kaggle: about seven terabytes spanning

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:37.645
three hundred and three domains,

00:02:37.725 --> 00:02:42.482
two hundred eighty-three thousand tables, and
roughly thirteen billion examples.

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:47.621
On average each table has about twenty-nine
numerical columns and eight textual ones,

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:51.550
with investing, finance, and economics among the
largest domains.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.573
For evaluation, they hold out twelve Kaggle tasks,

00:02:54.653 --> 00:02:58.503
six classification and six regression, never seen
in pretraining,

00:02:58.583 --> 00:03:03.340
plus seven widely used public benchmarks to compare
against established methods.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.321
The experiments show pretraining pays off.

00:03:06.401 --> 00:03:08.618
On seven standard public benchmarks,

00:03:08.698 --> 00:03:13.469
UniTabE reaches an average area-under-curve of
about zero point eight three,

00:03:13.549 --> 00:03:17.618
beating Tapas, FT-Transformer, and the industry
favorite XGBoost.

00:03:17.698 --> 00:03:22.469
On the twelve held-out Kaggle tasks, spanning
classification and regression,

00:03:22.549 --> 00:03:26.234
it again outperforms XGBoost and a strong TransTab
variant.

00:03:26.314 --> 00:03:28.787
It also performs well in zero-shot mode,

00:03:28.867 --> 00:03:33.766
making accurate predictions on some datasets with
no task-specific finetuning,

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:37.340
evidence of genuine transferable reasoning about tables.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.454
Ablations confirm each part earns its place.

00:03:40.534 --> 00:03:45.571
Removing the linking layer, which ties names to
values, causes the largest drop,

00:03:45.651 --> 00:03:49.920
from an AUC of zero point eight three down to zero
point seven five.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.398
Removing the fuse layer that injects data-type
information also hurts,

00:03:54.478 --> 00:03:56.125
and removing both is worse.

00:03:56.205 --> 00:04:01.369
Dropping either pretraining objective, multi-cell
masking or contrastive learning,

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:02.904
reduces performance too.

00:04:02.984 --> 00:04:06.742
Notably, a one-layer decoder beats three- or
six-layer ones,

00:04:06.822 --> 00:04:09.940
supporting the choice to keep the decoder shallow.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.505
The pretraining paradigm that reshaped language and
vision can extend to tabular data.

00:04:15.585 --> 00:04:22.301
The key is to respect table structure rather than
flatten it into text: represent each cell by its column name,

00:04:22.381 --> 00:04:27.506
value, and data type, refine with a Transformer,
and adapt through free-form prompts.

00:04:27.586 --> 00:04:28.914
Pretrained on billions,

00:04:28.994 --> 00:04:33.077
UniTabE becomes a general tabular model that
transfers across tasks,

00:04:33.157 --> 00:04:38.220
beats the XGBoost baseline, and handles missing
values and tables that gain columns.
